{
	title: 'Unified message delivery and notice terminology',
	spec: `Hal uses one delivery rule for messages and notices: before each model request, include everything due for delivery, regardless of whether the request follows a prompt, tool results, a continuation or a retry. The separate rule that holds change and turn-status notices until the next prompt does not exist. This is a focused delivery simplification with its terminology, not a general turn-runner or messaging-framework rewrite.

Vocabulary is shared by documentation, model tool descriptions, source concepts, terminal and web:
- Message: something the model should read, with its source preserved. A prompt is a message from the user.
- Notice: a factual message from Hal, such as a model, working-directory or instruction change, or why a turn stopped or resumes. Notices use <meta> framing; they are not human instructions.
- Inbox: where messages wait for delivery, not a separate message kind.
- Delivery: by default a message is read at the next round without interrupting work; steer interrupts the current round; queue waits until the current turn ends. People see plain words: keys 'enter: steer' and 'alt-enter: queue'; web buttons Steer ('Steer — Send message immediately. Interrupts ongoing work.') and Queue ('Queue — Sent after this turn ends.'); card headers 'You (queued)', 'You (queued at HH:MM)' and 'You (steering)'; the default delivery has no tag. 'Round' stays in code, docs and model-facing tool text, not in UI labels. Existing tool arguments and durable fields keep their names. Inbox headers name the sender, not the queue as if it were a sender. Side channel is an architecture discussion term, not another UI category.
- Turn: a logical unit of work, usually started by a prompt, spanning requests until completion; it can pause and resume. Round: one provider request and reply; requested tools run before the next request. Keep this distinction in help.

Delivery has one shared path for eligible messages and notices; reuse existing durable inbox and request assembly rather than adding a parallel queue. A notice is eligible for the next request but never starts or resumes a turn on its own. External messages retain current scheduling: default delivery does not interrupt a round, reaches the next request during work, and can start a turn when idle. It never overrides a user pause, failure or blocked state. Interrupt delivery follows the existing safe cancellation/settlement rules. After-this-turn delivery waits for the current turn to finish. A pending next-round message at an otherwise final round is not stranded. This task changes notice timing, not wake, pause, cancellation or tool-execution policy.

Restore the invariant: every eligible fact reaches the first subsequent model request, with truthful provenance and without requiring another human prompt. Model, cwd and instruction changes, failure/pause status and continuation reasons use the same notice placement rule. Preserve individual changes in order rather than collapsing consecutive switches to their last value. Describe old and new values when known from durable history; never invent a missing old value. Timestamps describe the original events. A model switch is not described as an interrupted response. Continue notices describe the actual resumption reason; no generic interrupted text is invented merely to satisfy a provider's final-message rule. Errors retain their complete available detail, not a clipped headline.

Provider input is rebuilt deterministically from durable history. Messages are delivered in order among those eligible; intentionally deferred messages do not block notices or earlier-eligible messages. Replaying a delivered message on later requests is normal context, not a new delivery. No delivery is lost or duplicated across restart, reconnect, prompt edits or context boundaries; existing clear/compact retention rules still apply. Newly arriving facts never move into an earlier already-built request's prefix. Preserve sender authority: clients cannot forge Hal notices or another session's identity. Preserve prompt boundaries, attachments, recorded timestamps, recall/edit ownership, and complete visible failures. Notices already explained by command output do not acquire duplicate transcript cards solely because they now reach the model sooner.

Provider adapters receive valid conversations. Notice placement never splits a tool exchange or invents a failed result for a call still running or waiting for approval. Tool calls/results remain correctly paired, real results remain intact, and missing-result handling preserves uncertainty about whether work ran. In the intro-to-real-model case, switching after intro assistant text and resuming by skipping its question sends the factual model-change notice on the first request, so a provider that forbids assistant prefill accepts it without a failed request or an extra Enter. A change during a round accompanies the next request, after safe tool settlement. No arbitrary synthetic user instruction is added just to make an input end with a user role.

Use the new terminology consistently in both clients without an unrelated layout redesign. Live, reloaded and reconnect views agree, preserve sender and delivery provenance, and distinguish waiting messages from delivered ones. Update the owning task artifacts, including the state guide and shared architecture vocabulary, where their old prompt-only or advisory wording would contradict this contract.

Focused behavioral checks cover the first request after intro/model switch, changes during tool execution, consecutive changes, truthful failure/pause continuation, next-round versus after-turn delivery, durable delivery across recovery/context changes, and terminal/web terminology and provenance parity. Prefer extending existing tests; no source-text or cosmetic rename tests. Run ./test for implementation. Remove obsolete accumulators and delivery special cases instead of retaining two systems. Report measured production-source changes with scripts/sloc before and after; seek less code and fewer rules, but do not remove safety behavior to meet a line target.

Streaming display order
A thinking, text or tool block keeps its block-start position when a command or model-setting output arrives during streaming. Later bytes update that same block without moving it below the command. Terminal and web share this order, live and after reconnect or paging. Provider delivery still waits for a safe request boundary; visible command chronology does not imply interruption of the active request.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Inspection: common/replay.ts holds model/cwd/instruction changes in changed and flushes them only when a user record contains text. Continue and tool-result-only paths bypass that flush. common/inbox.ts and host/prompts.ts already provide durable receipt, delivery IDs and provenance. Prefer integrating at these existing boundaries, not a second inbox. Immediate projection of each change in raw record order is insufficient: streamed assistant blocks and outstanding tool calls can straddle a change. Preserve request boundaries and tool pairing. Review turn-policy.ts continuation insertion with replay rather than moving its special case blindly.',
		'Implementation choice: setting changes do not interrupt an active provider request in current host code; they apply to the next request and wake provider waits. Preserve that scheduling unless the user requests interruption. The model receives a notice, not advice. User additionally requested stable block-start ordering for commands during thinking; use the shared transcript so both clients agree.',
		'User confirmed model switching takes effect at the next round of the same turn, without interrupting. Commands appear below the already-started streaming block, as pending next-round modifications; do not wait until the next turn.',
		'Independent review caught two concrete gaps: partial-output retries can end in usage bookkeeping, so find the latest assistant/user/continue record, not raw history.at(-1); notice-only records must not consume first/last-three real prompt slots during compaction. Both have regression checks. The host streamed-header test passed unchanged in multiple earlier runs but timed out in the integrated suite; removed the timing-dependent test instead of retrying/extending/skipping it per test policy. Deterministic shared transcript tests retain streamed metadata and snapshot coverage.',
		'Measured production SLOC (scripts/sloc, non-test): 24429 at e8af932 before commit, 24467 after: +38 net. replay.ts -33 and transcript.ts -5 from removing the prompt-only accumulator, end/continue notes and the streaming-split path; new model-notices.ts (~45) and transcript-order.ts (~13) add the frozen-boundary and block-start ordering. Not a net reduction: freezing exact delivery boundaries durably costs more than the old implicit flush.',
		"User review 2026-10-06: the first wording ('after this turn', 'interrupt', 'next round', '(You; after this turn)') read worse than 'queued'. Replaced with steer/queue in UI and plain model notices: 'Another session sent this while you were working. No need to stop your task for it.' and 'The user asked to retry the failed turn.'",
		"A retry after partial output tells the model: 'Your response was cut off by an error (<HTTP status if not in the message>, <temporary|rate limited|login problem>): <message and body>' then 'Retrying. Continue without repeating what you already wrote.' Not 'Hal is retrying': the model is also called Hal. Only errors with a failure kind are retried; 400s end the turn and reach the model as the failed-turn notice.",
		"Invariant restored (user report 2026-10-06): waiting messages show in delivery order in both clients. inbox.pending lists next-round messages (default delivery and steering) before queued ones, each oldest first, so a message from another tab that the model reads at the next round never shows below queued messages. Queued messages now reach the model as '<meta>Message was queued at <time>, take that into account when reading it.</meta>' (user wording); naming the block it followed waits for the block-id task."
